226:- F-318 **Under walk, the load check reads an own generic beside a functional method of a generic trait on their declared domains, `self` of a self-typed trait read as `C[\X\] & X` in the exclusion test.** It builds P-157 and P-152. Where the parameter-by-parameter check refuses an own generic function beside a functional method of a generic trait that declares no static parameters of its own, walk reads the pair as two own generics (`genericPairValid`), the trait's static parameters standing for the method's own; in `domainsExcludeTogether`'s meet, the self parameter of a trait self-typed directly (its parameter `X` bounded by the trait at `X`, no `comprises` clause or one that lists only `...`) excludes a type where the trait's instance or `X` does (`ProjectFortress/src/com/sun/fortress/interpreter/evaluator/values/OverloadedFunction.java`, `validOnDeclaredDomains`, `traitMethodPairValid`, `selfTypeParameter`). So a program's generic operators between an array of a number type and a number, beside a self-typed additive group's own, load and run as on the compiled path, and with `Number`'s `comprises` clause removed in a scratch copy walk loads and runs `BooleanOps`, `ArrayScalarExtension`, `ArrayOperatorsBesideLibrary` and `extendNumber`. A trait self-typed through another self-typed trait, and a method with static parameters of its own, are not read so and keep the refusal. Walk applies no extender rule of P-146, so an object that extends a self-typed trait at a type it is not a subtype of now loads and runs one of two fitting declarations (row 893); the checker refuses a generic function more specific than such a method that walk loads (row 894). Gated by `tests/ExclusionVectorScalarWalk`, `ExclusionArrayScalarLibraryShapeWalk`, `GenericBesideTraitMethodInsideWalk` and `GenericInsideTraitMethodWalk` (load and dispatch), and the refusals `ExclusionVectorScalarOverlapWalk` and `GenericInsideTraitMethodReturnRuleWalk`. Detail: `compile-ladder/rung-walk-vector-scalar/REPORT.md` sections 1, 2 and 4.
64:- P-152 **Walk and the compiled checker read the exclusion of two generic declarations over their whole parameter types, by existential reduction.** With static parameters, the parameter types of two declarations can exclude each other as a whole, even where no single parameter excludes its counterpart. In `o[\T\](x: P[\T\], b: Box[\T\])` beside `o[\T\](x: Q[\T\], b: Box[\T\])`, where `P[\T\]` excludes `Q[\T\]` only at the same `T`, neither parameter excludes alone. But no value is a `Box` of two types, so `b` fixes the same `T` for both declarations, and no argument fits both. A generic trait in `Box`'s place fixes `T` in the same way. Both paths read such a pair as excluding, and judge it on that reading by the overloading rules (P-45: the 2011 paper's No Duplicates, Meet and Return Type rules). Why: the type group's 2011 paper decides exclusion between parameter types with static parameters by existential reduction (`Papers/Types/exc-reduction.tick`), as the revised specification states (`Specification/advanced/overloading.tex`, "Declarations with Static Parameters"). Walk refuses such a pair at load where one declaration has a `where` clause, which the checker accepts (row 787); and walk reads an object type over a static parameter, such as `Box[\T\]`, as excluding a type that it equals at some instance, such as `Box[\ZZ32\]`, so that it loads a pair that the checker refuses (row 788).
68:- P-157 **`Vector` and `Matrix` stay additive groups, and the overloading check's exclusion test reads the self type and the static parameters' bounds, so the arrays' scalar `+` and `-` stand beside the vectors' own.** The team's `Vector` and `Matrix` extend `AdditiveGroup`, and the library's generic block of `+`, `-`, `MIN` and `MAX` between an array and a scalar (P-88) stays beside them. With symbolic operators checked (P-155), the checker refused 8 such pairs, though no call fits both: a vector's own `+` takes two vectors, the block's `+` an array and a number, and arrays exclude numbers. So the exclusion test reads a self-typed trait's `self` as `C[\X\] & X`, the fact that P-146's extender rule guarantees, and its existential reduction reads the static parameters' bounds (P-152); specificity and the Return Type Rule keep the plain signatures. Why: it keeps both the team's library as written and the revival's block, and it was his first stance; taking vectors out of `AdditiveGroup` would have bent the team's design for a gap of the checker. Reading bounds is the POPL 2019 paper's own reduction. Probe A1 measured it (`perf-probes/self-type-exclusion/REPORT.md`): the 8 gone, nothing else moved.
13:**Where a declaration has static parameters, exclusion is read over the whole tuple of parameter types (P-152).** By the exclusion rule, a value of both domains has one instance of each generic type at each position, so those instances' static arguments are equal. The domains exclude where these equalities cannot hold together, or where some position excludes at the instance that they force. So `o[\T\](x: P[\T\], b: Box[\T\])` beside `o[\T\](x: Q[\T\], b: Box[\T\])`, with `P[\T\] excludes { Q[\T\] }`, is valid. Both paths refuse a pair where the equalities leave a static parameter free, as `o[\T, U\](x: Q[\U\], b: Box[\T\])` beside the first `o`: nothing ties `U` to `T`. In its test of whether two domains exclude each other, the checker puts the meet of a static parameter's declared bounds in the parameter's place where the parameter is a whole parameter type, an element of a tuple or a member of an intersection or union (P-157). Inside a static argument, as `T` in `Box[\T\]`, the parameter stays.

